# Clean architecture — non-negotiable

Canonical guides: [`/CLAUDE.md`](../../CLAUDE.md) (repo summary + AI doc map), [`/booking-service/CLAUDE.md`](../../booking-service/CLAUDE.md) (service depth), [`/docs/ai/README.md`](../../docs/ai/README.md) (CRUD unit-test checklist for AI). On drift, those win.

## Dependency direction

```
interface  →  application  →  domain  ←  infrastructure
```

- **Domain** depends on nothing. No `@nestjs/*`, no `@prisma/client`, no `ioredis`, no DTOs, no HTTP types. Pure TypeScript only.
- **Application** depends only on domain. Defines and consumes ports (interfaces). Never imports a concrete adapter.
- **Infrastructure** implements ports. Houses `@prisma/client`, `ioredis`, `redlock`, `kafkajs`, HTTP clients, and in-process mocks of upstream services.
- **Interface** depends on application. Controllers map HTTP shapes to use-case inputs/outputs.

If an inner layer needs an outer layer, you have the dependency wrong. Invert it: define a port in the inner layer and let the outer layer implement it.

## Storage rules

- **PostgreSQL is the source of truth.** Redis is cache + lock store, never source of truth.
- **Cache-aside, not write-through.** Reads: GET Redis → on miss SELECT PG → SET back with TTL. Writes: PG first, then invalidate the affected cache key.
- Prisma owns the schema and migrations (`prisma/schema.prisma`). No second migration system.
- `@prisma/client` types never leak past `infrastructure/`. Map to domain entities in `persistence/mappers/`.

## NestJS conventions

- Constructor injection with `private readonly`. No property injection.
- Inject ports via DI tokens (`@Inject(BOOKING_REPOSITORY)`). Never inject concrete classes into application code.
- Singleton scope unless there's a concrete reason. Request-scoped providers disable the DI cache.
- Global `ValidationPipe` with `whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true, transform: true`.
- Call `app.enableShutdownHooks()` so adapters can implement `OnModuleDestroy`.

## Errors

- Domain throws plain typed errors (`SlotAlreadyBookedError`, `LockNotAcquiredError`). **Never** throw `HttpException` from domain or application.
- Interface layer's exception filter is the single mapping point from domain errors → HTTP.

## Code style

- File names kebab-case. One primary export per file. No barrel `index.ts` re-exports.
- Async/await everywhere. ESLint `no-floating-promises` is on — `await` or explicit `void`.
- Default to no comments. Add one only when the *why* is non-obvious. Don't narrate what the code does.

## Unit tests — CRUD aggregates

When adding or changing an aggregate CRUD slice under `booking-service/`, ship **`test/fakes/fake-<aggregate>.repository.ts`** and **`src/application/<aggregate>/<aggregate>.service.spec.ts`** (hand-written fake, no Prisma mocks). Checklist: [`/docs/ai/README.md`](../../docs/ai/README.md).

## Don'ts (across the repo)

- Don't put business logic in controllers, repositories, or NestJS modules.
- Don't reuse a domain entity as an HTTP DTO. Don't reuse a Prisma model as a domain entity.
- Don't add abstractions ahead of need. Three similar lines beats a premature abstraction.
- Don't add new infra (queue, cache layer, datastore) without it appearing in the architecture doc.
- Don't disable lint rules to make code pass. Fix the underlying issue.
- Don't grow business logic in mock adapters. If a mock needs branches, the test belongs elsewhere.
